export const howHTML = () => `<h1>How the town watches</h1><div class="cols"><div>
  <h2>He is seen</h2><ul><li>Someone sees you if they are facing you and you are in light. Light sets the distance: under a sodium lamp about 35 metres, in a dark doorway about 6.</li><li>There is no meter. The corners of the picture are the same number the town uses: they lift when you stand in light and close in when you are in shadow. Lamp pools on the ground are the rule made visible.</li><li>Noise depends on the hour. At noon a footstep carries 2 metres; at 3 a.m. it carries 8, and running carries 20. Rain is cover. You are told this by sound alone: your footsteps, a car door, a shout are louder the further they carry, and go under in rain and the noon street. A noise at night can bring a neighbour to a window, and the window lights up.</li><li>How well someone knows you decides whether they can name you. A stranger sees a man; a neighbour at the same distance sees Tom.</li><li>The runner's coat [C] makes you much harder to name after dark, and is a thing people remark on in daylight. Anyone who sees you in it by day can join it to what they saw at night.</li><li>The cab is always described, whoever is driving. At night that is cover: Mickey's has a night driver too.</li><li>Nothing is marked over anybody's head. Watch their bodies: a head that turns to you, a longer look, a pause in a walk, two people who glance your way while they talk, someone stepping back into a doorway, a figure at a window with the curtain pulled aside.</li><li>You know a person by the street's description of them until you hear their name.</li></ul>
  <h2>It is written down, it spreads, it comes back</h2><ul><li>Every named person keeps what they saw or heard for good: who, what, where, when, how sure.</li><li>Stories pass when people meet on their routines: breakfast at the caff, the rank, the fish queue, the chapel. A story fades only when nobody feeds it.</li><li>Each person says a thing to your face once, in their own words, while it is news.</li><li>What the street calls you is how well it knows you, not how much it likes you: the new owner, Nowak, Tom, Tommy. You find it out from what people say to you and about you, and it goes in your Ledger.</li><li>The Ledger [L] is only what you believe: by person, by event, and what is open. When you come to believe something new, a line says so low at the left.</li></ul></div><div>
  <h2>The keys</h2><ul><li>W A S D move, Shift run, mouse looks (click to hold the mouse; Esc frees it). Wheel moves the camera in and out.</li><li>E use, talk, doors. T the second thing on offer (tea, a paper).</li><li>In a talk: type your own words and press Enter, or press Tab for suggested lines, Up and Down to choose, Enter to say the one in hand, Tab again for your own words. The slip from your Ledger starts folded beside the text box: F2 unfolds it, and it stays as you left it. Esc leaves.</li><li>F in and out of the cab. In the cab: W S A D, Space handbrake, Y takes a fare.</li><li>L opens the Ledger anywhere, and L or Esc closes it. Tab is only for the suggested lines in a talk. In the Ledger: Q and E change part, Up and Down move along the entries, Left and Right turn the page, a letter jumps to a name, and / then a letter jumps to any letter (E, L and Q too). A pad plays the whole game: left stick walks and steers, right stick looks, right trigger runs or drives, left trigger brakes; A is E, X is T, Y is the cab, B the coat, LB fronts up, RB picks up, D-pad up takes a fare, Menu pauses, View opens the Ledger (there LB and RB change part, the D-pad moves, A marks, B closes). In a written choice the D-pad moves and A takes it; in a talk Y brings the suggested lines. Typing your own words still needs the keyboard.</li><li>C coat. G picks up what is at hand.</li><li>Hold Z to lean on a wall and let time pass. Beds and the cot skip to evening, the last ferry, or morning.</li><li>Q fronts up to whoever is in front of you. R steps up a rung, X walks away. Stare, square up, threaten, shove, strike, weapon out.</li><li>If it comes to blows: left mouse or J to hit (hold for a heavy one), right mouse or K to block, Space to step back. A miss leaves you open.</li><li>Esc or P pauses.</li></ul>
  <h2>Money</h2><ul><li>Pocket is clean: fares. The tin is dirty: night work. Sheila can put the tin through the fare book, but only so much hides behind the fares you have actually driven.</li><li>Some people will take money for what they see. Some will be insulted.</li></ul>
  <h2>You cannot lose</h2><p>Refusing ends an arrangement, never the game. Beaten down, you wake at Grace's. Taken in, you lose hours and money. The week ends on Friday with Sheila's question.</p></div></div><div class="row"><button class="btn" data-act="back">Back</button></div>`;

